WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.500
A join pairs rows that match.

2
00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:11.204
Its meaning is a loop: for each payment, look at the shops, and keep the pairs whose shop numbers are equal.

3
00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:12.968
Payment 3 has shop 4.

4
00:00:12.968 --> 00:00:18.268
So it checks shop 1, shop 2, shop 3, and finds its match at the fourth.

5
00:00:18.268 --> 00:00:20.807
Four comparisons, for one payment.

6
00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:23.476
With four shops, that's nothing.

7
00:00:23.476 --> 00:00:28.449
The course's large data set has a million payments and 2,500 shops.

8
00:00:28.449 --> 00:00:35.576
With the faster methods switched off, PostgreSQL joined only the first 10,000 payments this way.

9
00:00:35.576 --> 00:00:39.362
That took 0.44 seconds and 6.3 million comparisons.

10
00:00:39.362 --> 00:00:43.000
All million payments would take about 40 seconds.

11
00:00:43.167 --> 00:00:45.716
The answer must be what the loop produces.

12
00:00:45.716 --> 00:00:49.175
But does the database have to do all that work to get it?

13
00:00:49.567 --> 00:00:51.476
Go back to the party's cloakroom.

14
00:00:51.476 --> 00:00:57.089
At the end of the night, a queue of guests wants their coats, and the clerk has a box of tickets.

15
00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:00.342
A slow clerk reads the whole box for every guest.

16
00:01:00.342 --> 00:01:03.756
By the hundredth guest, the queue reaches the car park.

17
00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:07.328
A smart clerk does some work before the doors open.

18
00:01:07.328 --> 00:01:14.353
She files each ticket into one of four pigeonholes, by one rule: the remainder when you divide its number by 4.

19
00:01:14.567 --> 00:01:18.904
Then each guest's number points to one pigeonhole, and she looks only there.

20
00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:19.889
Let's map it.

21
00:01:19.889 --> 00:01:22.622
The tickets are the shops, the smaller input.

22
00:01:22.622 --> 00:01:25.111
The guests in the queue are the payments.

23
00:01:25.111 --> 00:01:28.087
And the number each one holds is the shop number.

24
00:01:28.233 --> 00:01:30.500
Let's file our four shops.

25
00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:34.773
Shop 1: 1 divided by 4 leaves 1, so pigeonhole 1.

26
00:01:34.773 --> 00:01:37.737
Shop 2 goes in 2, and shop 3 in 3.

27
00:01:37.737 --> 00:01:38.697
And shop 4?

28
00:01:38.697 --> 00:01:42.795
4 divided by 4 leaves nothing, so pigeonhole 0.

29
00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:44.400
That's the first phase.

30
00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:47.073
The shops are read once, and never again.

31
00:01:47.333 --> 00:01:49.554
Now the payments, one at a time.

32
00:01:49.554 --> 00:01:53.995
Payment 1 has shop 1: pigeonhole 1, compare the number, a match.

33
00:01:53.995 --> 00:01:58.020
Out comes one row, and it never looked at shops 2, 3 or 4.

34
00:01:58.133 --> 00:02:01.319
Payment 2 goes to pigeonhole 2, the same way.

35
00:02:01.533 --> 00:02:03.317
Payment 3 has shop 4.

36
00:02:03.317 --> 00:02:04.760
Which pigeonhole?

37
00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:06.119
Pause and guess.

38
00:02:09.367 --> 00:02:10.336
Pigeonhole 0.

39
00:02:10.336 --> 00:02:14.436
One look, one comparison, and there's Volt Electronics.

40
00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:15.694
Now payment 5.

41
00:02:15.694 --> 00:02:18.974
It has shop 5, which isn't among our four.

42
00:02:18.974 --> 00:02:25.223
5 divided by 4 leaves 1, so it lands in pigeonhole 1, next to Northwind Grocers.

43
00:02:25.367 --> 00:02:29.986
If the pigeonhole were enough, payment 5 would be paired with Northwind.

44
00:02:29.986 --> 00:02:30.692
Wrong shop.

45
00:02:30.867 --> 00:02:37.749
So the clerk always checks the number on the ticket: 1 isn't 5, no match, and an inner join puts out nothing.

46
00:02:37.749 --> 00:02:39.832
The pigeonhole gives a shortlist.

47
00:02:39.832 --> 00:02:41.916
The number on the ticket decides.

48
00:02:42.267 --> 00:02:43.199
Now the names.

49
00:02:43.199 --> 00:02:47.992
The filing rule is a hash function: it turns a key into a bucket number.

50
00:02:47.992 --> 00:02:51.853
The rack is a hash table, and each pigeonhole is a bucket.

51
00:02:51.853 --> 00:02:54.382
Filing the smaller input is the build.

52
00:02:54.382 --> 00:02:56.845
Looking up each payment is the probe.

53
00:02:56.845 --> 00:02:58.776
Together, that's a hash join.

54
00:02:58.867 --> 00:03:05.650
On the large data set, the hash table held all 2,500 shops in about 130 kilobytes.

55
00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:14.418
Then one pass over the million payments: 174 milliseconds on one core, and 67 with two workers sharing it.

56
00:03:14.418 --> 00:03:17.148
The loop needed about 40 seconds.

57
00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:18.412
Three limits.

58
00:03:18.412 --> 00:03:22.199
Pigeonholes can only answer one question: is it equal?

59
00:03:22.199 --> 00:03:26.267
So a hash join needs an equals sign in the join condition.

60
00:03:26.467 --> 00:03:35.497
The rack has to fit in the memory the work_mem setting allows, 4 megabytes by default, or PostgreSQL splits the work into batches on disk.

61
00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:41.333
And there are other fast ways, such as looking each shop up in an index, a sorted catalogue.

62
00:03:41.333 --> 00:03:49.047
The planner, the part of PostgreSQL that decides how to run a query, estimates the cost of each, and EXPLAIN shows its choice.

63
00:03:49.267 --> 00:03:51.512
So, does the database run the loop?

64
00:03:51.512 --> 00:03:53.565
The loop says what a join means.

65
00:03:53.565 --> 00:03:55.746
The planner decides how to run it.

66
00:03:55.746 --> 00:04:00.493
File the small side once, and send each row of the big side to one bucket.

67
00:04:00.933 --> 00:04:02.066
One quick check.

68
00:04:02.066 --> 00:04:04.119
A payment for shop 8 arrives.

69
00:04:04.119 --> 00:04:06.739
Which pigeonhole, and what comes out?

70
00:04:06.739 --> 00:04:07.872
Pause and think.

71
00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:15.721
8 divided by 4 leaves 0: pigeonhole 0, where Volt sits.

72
00:04:15.721 --> 00:04:18.409
4 isn't 8, so nothing comes out.
